      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Oxaprozin has nearly complete oral absorption and high systemic availability in humans, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C15"],
      "claim": "Meclofenamate has oral bioavailability above 85% in humans, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C13"],
      "claim": "SSI-4, a small molecule with a chlorophenyl group, shows excellent oral bioavailability in PDX models, supporting high F.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a benzodiazepine N-oxide (chlordiazepoxide-like). The most transferable evidence—clobazam (90% F), trazodone (63–91% F), encorafenib (85% F), oxaprozin (high), meclofenamate (>85% F), and SSI-4 (excellent)—strongly supports high oral bioavailability. Low-F analogues (loperamide, batimastat, clofazimine) have negligible transfer scores and distinct absorption/efflux liabilities. Therefore predict high.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule in humans.",
    "No data on the effect of the N-oxide moiety on absorption or first-pass metabolism.",
    "No data on P-gp or other transporter involvement for the query.",
    "No fed/fasted comparison for the query itself."
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [],
  "revision_action": "initial"
}

